# Creating pull requests (personal)

This file is **local to this machine**. Copy it to
`~/.agents/rules/creating-pull-requests.mdc` and symlink
`~/.cursor/rules/creating-pull-requests.mdc` to that path. Do not commit it
into an application repo.

Use the **`gh` command** via the Shell tool for GitHub tasks (issues, pull
requests, checks, releases).

Packet idea: [How to Review AI-Generated Pull Requests](https://www.aibuilderclub.com/blog/reviewing-ai-generated-pull-requests).

## When this applies

Whenever you **draft, create, or update a pull request description**, including:

- `gh pr create` / `gh pr edit` / `gh pr ready`
- GitHub MCP create/update PR
- Repo `create-pr` skills
- The user asks to write a PR description, draft a PR body, or open a PR

Do **not** use the TodoWrite or Task tools for the PR-create ritual.

## Draft default

Open PRs as **draft** unless the user explicitly asks for a ready-for-review PR
in the same turn. Say one line when you use the exception.

## PR body format (required)

Every PR description must include **Goal**, **Ran**, and **Doubt**. **Test plan**
is optional and does not replace them. Placeholder comments alone do not count.

| Section | Required | Content |
|---------|----------|---------|
| **Goal** | Yes | What changed and why, in your words — not the file list |
| **Ran** | Yes | Commands/checks you ran and outcomes — not "CI is green" alone |
| **Doubt** | Yes | Where a miss would hurt; what you still do not know |
| **Test plan** | Optional | Checklist only |

**Hard gates**

- If the open repo's `AGENTS.md` or `.cursor/rules/` names different PR
  headings, use those. Do not use Goal, Ran, or Doubt in that repo.
- Do not open or finalize a PR body missing Goal, Ran, and Doubt with real content
  when this personal format applies.
- When updating an existing PR, preserve or add the three sections.
- Never drop Goal, Ran, or Doubt for a shorter description or test checklist alone.

## When the user asks you to create a pull request

1. Gather git status, diff, and commits since the base branch.
2. Draft a concise title (why, not what) and a body with Goal, Ran, and Doubt.
3. Create only if the user asked. Push only if they asked to push. Use `--draft`
   unless they asked for ready in the same turn.

```bash
gh pr create --draft --title "..." --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
## Goal

<!-- What changed and why, in your words. Not the file list. -->

## Ran

<!-- Commands/checks and outcomes. Not only CI green. -->

## Doubt

<!-- Where a miss would hurt; what you still do not know. -->

## Test plan

<!-- Optional checklist. Does not replace Goal, Ran, Doubt. -->
EOF
)"
```

Branch names: follow the **open repo's** `CONTRIBUTING.md` / `AGENTS.md`. If the
current name is wrong, stop and ask. Do not rename the branch on GitHub.

## Git safety

- NEVER update the git config
- NEVER run destructive git commands unless the user explicitly requests them
- NEVER skip hooks unless the user explicitly requests it or repo policy allows
- NEVER force push to main/master; warn if requested
- DO NOT push unless the user explicitly asks
- DO NOT commit unless the user explicitly asks
- Treat PR creation as permission to open a PR, not as permission to push,
  unless the user also asked to push

## Cloud

Cursor Cloud Agents do **not** load `~/.agents/` or `~/.cursor/rules/`. If a
team wants this format on Cloud, put it in the repo harness, a project rule, or
Team Rules. See the workshop file `pr-author-packet-rule.md`.
